.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Transform from tree-local 'reference' coordinate system to global 'physical space' coordinates\&. 

These are used in \fBp4est_vtk\&.h\fP to write global coordinate meshes to disk\&.
.PP
We provide several example geometries for use\&. You may also implement your own geometry as you see fit\&.
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
For geometry purposes, each tree has the local coordinate system $[0,1]^d$\&. For legacy/\fBp8est\fP compatibility reasons the local coordinates are always represented as a triple abc[3]\&. For a 2D quadtree mesh the local coordinates are abc[0] and abc[1] and the third coordinate abc[2] should be ignored\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SH "Typedef Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "typedef void(* p4est_geometry_destroy_t) (\fBp4est_geometry_t\fP *geom)"

.PP
Destructor prototype for a user-allocated \fBp4est_geometry_t\fP\&. It is invoked by \fBp4est_geometry_destroy\fP\&. If the user chooses to reserve the structure statically, there is no need to provide it\&. 
.SS "typedef void(* p4est_geometry_X_t) (\fBp4est_geometry_t\fP *geom, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP which_tree, const double abc[3], double xyz[3])"

.PP
Forward transformation from the tree-local coordinates to physical space\&. 
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
The two-dimensional connectivities built into \fBp4est\fP have 3D vertex coordinates that can be used in the transformation if so desired\&. However, connectivities are not in general required to have vertex coordinate information\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIgeom\fP associated geometry 
.br
\fIwhich_tree\fP tree id inside forest 
.br
\fIabc\fP tree-local coordinates: $[0,1]^d$\&. For 2D meshes abc[2] should never be accessed\&. 
.br
\fIxyz\fP cartesian coordinates in physical space after geometry 
.RE
.PP

