.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "void p4est_geometry_connectivity_X (\fBp4est_geometry_t\fP * geom, \fBp4est_topidx_t\fP which_tree, const double abc[3], double xyz[3])"

.PP
Geometric coordinate transformation for geometry created with \fBp4est_geometry_new_connectivity\fP\&. This is defined by tri/binlinear interpolation from vertex coordinates\&.
.PP
May also be used as a building block in custom geometric coordinate transforms\&. See for example \fBp4est_geometry_new_sphere2d\fP or \fBp4est_geometry_new_disk2d\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIgeom\fP associated geometry 
.br
\fIwhich_tree\fP tree id inside forest 
.br
\fIabc\fP tree-local reference coordinates : [0,1]^3\&. Note: abc[2] is only accessed by the P4_TO_P8 version 
.br
\fIxyz\fP Cartesian coordinates in physical space after geometry
.RE
.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
The associated geometry is assumed to have a connectivity as its *user field, and this connectivity is assumed to have vertex information in its *tree_to_vertex field\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void p4est_geometry_coordinates_lnodes (\fBp4est_t\fP * p4est, \fBp4est_lnodes_t\fP * lnodes, const double * refloc, \fBp4est_geometry_t\fP * geom, sc_array_t * coordinates, sc_array_t * element_coordinates)"

.PP
Compute node coordinates for a \fBp4est_lnodes\fP structure\&. Presently we allow for an lnodes degree of 1 or 2\&. Cubic or higher degrees may be transparently enabled in the future\&.
.PP
The simple mode assigns one tree reference coordinate to each lnode\&. This may not be suitable for visualizing periodic connectivities\&.
.PP
In a more advanced mode indicated by NULL \fCelement_coordinates\fP input, the coordinates are made unique by reference location: If a tree is periodic, for example, its corners reference the same lnode but will generate separate coordinate entries for proper visualization\&. There will be more coordinates generated than there are lnodes\&.
.PP
The coordinate numbers generated by the present version of the function are partition-dependent\&. This may be seen as a flaw\&. Looking into it\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fI\fBp4est\fP\fP A valid forest structure\&. 
.br
\fIlnodes\fP A valid \fBp4est_lnodes\fP structure of degree 1 or 2\&. Higher degrees not presently allowed\&. Must be derived from the \fC\fBp4est\fP\fP\&. 
.br
\fIrefloc\fP Eventually used for cubic and upwards degrees\&. We will expect degree + 1 many values for the one-dimensional reference node spacing\&. Out of these, the indices from 1 to (degree - 1) / 2 inclusive will be accessed by this function\&. The others default by symmetry considerations\&. 
.br
\fIgeom\fP May be NULL for generating the tree-reference coordinates, or a valid geometry object for transforming the reference into mapped space\&. 
.br
\fIcoordinates\fP On input, an array with entries of 3 double variables each\&. Resized in this function and populated with coordinate tuples\&. With a NULL geometry, these are in [0, 1]**2\&. Otherwise, they are mapped by the geometry\&. 
.br
\fIelement_coordinates\fP This may be NULL, in which case we generate one coordinate tuple for each lnode\&. Otherwise, this must be an array with entries of type \fBp4est_locidx_t\fP\&. Is resized to the same number of entries as \fClnodes->element_nodes\fP\&. Its entries point into the coordinates array\&. The tree index of any given entry is implicit in that this array is derived from a \fBp4est\fP, where sets of (degree + 1)**2 entries each correspond to the forest elements in order\&. 
.RE
.PP

